{"no":3,"file_no":3,"title":"Phonetics","area":"English","hours":"2","lang":"en","words":2169,"read_min":12,"figures":3,"examples":0,"has_questions":true,"toc":[{"id":"s1","title":"3.2 Phonemes (vowels and consonants)","kind":""},{"id":"s2","title":"3.3 Syllables and stress (word and sentence)","kind":""},{"id":"s3","title":"Formula and fact sheet","kind":""},{"id":"s4","title":"Chapter summary","kind":""},{"id":"s5","title":"Practice questions","kind":"qs"}],"html":"<p>Phonetics carries about <strong>2 of the 16 English questions</strong> (our estimate), and the official model paper shows exactly what they look like: <em>\"The word 'procrastination' has its stress on the … syllable\"</em> and <em>\"Which of the following pairs have the same initial vowel sound? (ago, alone)\"</em>. Both are answerable with a short rule set and a little practice reading phonetic values aloud.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\">\n<div class=\"bt\">What the paper asks from this chapter</div>\n<ul>\n<li>The 44 phonemes of Received Pronunciation: 20 vowels (12 pure + 8 diphthongs) and 24 consonants.</li>\n<li>Same-sound and different-sound word pairs (initial vowel, final consonant, silent letters).</li>\n<li>Number of syllables and the syllable that carries the primary stress.</li>\n<li>Stress shift between noun and verb, and sentence stress on content words.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"s1\">3.2 Phonemes (vowels and consonants)</h2>\n<p>English spelling is not phonetic, so we count <strong>sounds</strong>, not letters. A <em>phoneme</em> is the smallest unit of sound that changes meaning (<em>ship</em> /ʃɪp/ versus <em>sheep</em> /ʃiːp/ — the pair is a <strong>minimal pair</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Vowels (20).</strong> Twelve <strong>monophthongs</strong> (single, steady sounds): short /ɪ e æ ʌ ɒ ʊ ə/ and long /iː ɑː ɔː uː ɜː/. Eight <strong>diphthongs</strong> (glides): /eɪ aɪ ɔɪ aʊ əʊ ɪə eə ʊə/. Examples:</p>\n<table class=\"ctr\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Words</th>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>/iː/</td>\n<td>seat, feel, key</td>\n<td>/ɪ/</td>\n<td>sit, inn, village</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/e/</td>\n<td>bed, head, said</td>\n<td>/æ/</td>\n<td>cat, apple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɑː/</td>\n<td>car, father, calm</td>\n<td>/ʌ/</td>\n<td>cup, up, love</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɒ/</td>\n<td>hot, on, watch</td>\n<td>/ɔː/</td>\n<td>all, caught, door, more</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ʊ/</td>\n<td>put, foot, good</td>\n<td>/uː/</td>\n<td>food, blue, rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɜː/</td>\n<td>bird, learn, her</td>\n<td>/ə/</td>\n<td><strong>a</strong>go, <strong>a</strong>lone, sof<strong>a</strong> (schwa)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/eɪ/</td>\n<td>day, break, eight</td>\n<td>/aɪ/</td>\n<td>my, high, buy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɔɪ/</td>\n<td>boy, coin</td>\n<td>/aʊ/</td>\n<td>now, <strong>ow</strong>l, house</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/əʊ/</td>\n<td>go, home, boat</td>\n<td>/ɪə/</td>\n<td>here, ear, beer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>schwa /ə/</strong> is the commonest vowel sound in English and occurs only in unstressed syllables: <em>ago</em> /əˈgəʊ/ and <em>alone</em> /əˈləʊn/ begin with the same sound, while <em>owl</em> /aʊl/ and <em>all</em> /ɔːl/, <em>inn</em> /ɪn/ and <em>eel</em> /iːl/, <em>up</em> /ʌp/ and <em>on</em> /ɒn/ do not.</p><p class=\"ck\">Vowel <em>length</em> matters: <em>bit</em> /ɪ/ versus <em>beat</em> /iː/; <em>pull</em> /ʊ/ versus <em>pool</em> /uː/.</p>\n<p><strong>Consonants (24).</strong> Plosives /p b t d k g/; fricatives /f v θ ð s z ʃ ʒ h/; affricates /tʃ dʒ/; nasals /m n ŋ/; liquids /l r/; glides /j w/. Voiceless versus voiced pairs: /p b/, /t d/, /k g/, /f v/, /θ ð/, /s z/, /ʃ ʒ/, /tʃ dʒ/.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>/θ/ (voiceless) in <strong>th</strong>in, <strong>th</strong>ink, ba<strong>th</strong>; /ð/ (voiced) in <strong>th</strong>is, <strong>th</strong>en, ba<strong>th</strong>e.</li>\n<li>/ʃ/ in <strong>sh</strong>ip, na<strong>ti</strong>on, <strong>s</strong>ure; /ʒ/ in vi<strong>si</strong>on, mea<strong>s</strong>ure, plea<strong>s</strong>ure; /tʃ/ in <strong>ch</strong>air, <strong>t</strong>ure; /dʒ/ in <strong>j</strong>ug, <strong>g</strong>eneral, ba<strong>dg</strong>e.</li>\n<li>/ŋ/ in si<strong>ng</strong>, thi<strong>n</strong>k; /h/ in <strong>h</strong>at; /j/ in <strong>y</strong>es, <strong>u</strong>se.</li>\n<li><strong>Silent letters:</strong> k in <em>knife, know</em>; b in <em>climb, comb, doubt, debt</em>; w in <em>write, answer</em>; p in <em>psychology, pneumonia</em>; g in <em>gnat, sign</em>; h in <em>hour, honest</em>; l in <em>walk, calm</em>; t in <em>listen, castle</em>; n in <em>autumn, column</em>.</li>\n<li>The same letter can give different sounds: <em>c</em> in <em>cat</em> /k/ and <em>city</em> /s/; <em>s</em> in <em>sun</em> /s/ and <em>rose</em> /z/; <em>-ed</em> is /t/ after voiceless sounds (<em>walked</em>), /d/ after voiced (<em>played</em>), /ɪd/ after /t d/ (<em>wanted</em>); <em>-s</em> is /s/, /z/ or /ɪz/ (<em>cats, dogs, buses</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"s2\">3.3 Syllables and stress (word and sentence)</h2>\n<p>A <strong>syllable</strong> has one vowel sound: <em>beau-ti-ful</em> (3), <em>ed-u-ca-tion</em> (4), <em>com-mu-ni-ca-tion</em> (5), <em>pro-cras-ti-na-tion</em> (5). Count vowel <em>sounds</em>: <em>the-ater</em> is 3, <em>name</em> is 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Word stress</strong> is the syllable pronounced louder, longer and higher. Rules of thumb:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Words ending <strong>-tion, -sion, -ic, -ical, -ity, -ify, -ian, -ious</strong> stress the syllable <strong>before</strong> the ending: <em>edu-CA-tion, pro-cras-ti-NA-tion, eco-NOM-ic, ca-PA-ci-ty, pho-TOG-ra-pher</em>.</li>\n<li>Words ending <strong>-graphy, -logy, -ate, -ment</strong> (long words): <em>pho-TOG-ra-phy, bi-OL-o-gy, ECO-no-my</em> → <em>e-CON-o-my</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-syllable nouns and adjectives</strong> are usually stressed on the first syllable (<em>TAble, PREsent, REcord</em>); <strong>two-syllable verbs</strong> on the second (<em>preSENT, reCORD, deCIDE</em>). Stress shift marks the change of word class: <em>a PREsent</em> / <em>to preSENT</em>; <em>an INcrease</em> / <em>to inCREASE</em>; <em>a REcord</em> / <em>to reCORD</em>; <em>an OBject</em> / <em>to obJECT</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compound nouns</strong> are stressed on the first part (<em>BLACKboard, GREENhouse</em>); compound adjectives and verbs on the second (<em>bad-TEMpered, underSTAND</em>).</li>\n<li>Prefixes are generally unstressed (<em>re-TURN, un-HAPpy</em>); suffixes <em>-ful, -less, -ness, -ing</em> do not move the stress (<em>BEAUtiful</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sentence stress.</strong> In connected speech, <strong>content words</strong> (nouns, main verbs, adjectives, adverbs, negatives, question words) carry the stress; <strong>function words</strong> (articles, prepositions, auxiliaries, pronouns, conjunctions) are weak and their vowels reduce to /ə/.</p><p class=\"ck\"><em>I <strong>want</strong> to <strong>go</strong> to the <strong>market</strong>.</em> Stress changes the focus: <em>I didn't <strong>steal</strong> the money</em> (I did something else) versus <em>I didn't steal <strong>the</strong> money</em> (another money).</p><p class=\"ck\"><strong>Intonation:</strong> a falling tune for statements, wh-questions and commands; a rising tune for yes/no questions and uncertain lists (the last item of a list falls).</p>\n<div class=\"box caution\">\n<div class=\"bt\">Phonetics traps</div>\n<p>Count sounds, not letters (<em>knife</em> starts with /n/, <em>hour</em> with a vowel, so <em>an hour</em>). <em>-ed</em> has three sounds. Pairs that look alike do not sound alike: <em>cough, though, through, tough, thought</em>. The <strong>stress</strong> of a long word follows its <em>ending</em>, not its beginning.</p>\n</div>\n<p><figure class=\"nobreak photo grid\"><table class=\"pgrid\"><tbody><tr><td class=\"gc\"><img class=\"gp\" src=\"/static/web/study/6a1d5efcbcf97a561423.webp\" alt=\"photograph\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(a)</b> A spectrogram of one vowel: dark bands are its formants</div></td><td class=\"gc\"><img src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" alt=\"diagram\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(b)</b> Vowel chart from F1 and F2 (Peterson and Barney means)</div></td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption><b>Plate 3.1.</b> Vowels as two numbers. (a) A spectrogram of the vowel in see: each dark band is a formant, a frequency where the vocal tract strengthens the sound. (b) The first two formants fix the vowel. F1 rises as the tongue is lowered, and F2 falls as it moves back and the lips round. Plotted this way, nine English vowels (average adult male values from Peterson and Barney, 1952, rounded) form the same chart as the classroom vowel quadrilateral: i is highest and most front, with F1 = 270 Hz and F2 = 2290 Hz, while the open back vowel in pot has F1 = 730 Hz and F2 = 1090 Hz.<span class=\"src\">Photo credits: (a) Lee Ball, CC0.</span></figcaption></figure></p>\n<p><figure class=\"nobreak photo grid\"><table class=\"pgrid\"><tbody><tr><td class=\"gc\"><img class=\"gp\" src=\"/static/web/study/74d89095acbb5d8d9206.webp\" alt=\"photograph\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(a)</b> A metronome: English stressed syllables fall at roughly even beats</div></td><td class=\"gc\"><img src=\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\" alt=\"diagram\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(b)</b> Same three stresses, 3, 6 and 9 syllables, equal time</div></td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption><b>Plate 3.2.</b> Sentence stress and rhythm. (a) A metronome ticks at an even rate; English speech approximates this with its stressed syllables, while the unstressed ones are squeezed in between. (b) Three sentences carry the same three stressed words (dogs, chasing, cats) but have 3, 6 and 9 syllables, counted by a vowel-group rule. At an assumed 120 beats per minute (0.5 s per stress), the stress-timed model gives 1.5 s for every one of them, whereas a syllable-timed language at 0.25 s per syllable would need 0.75, 1.5 and 2.25 s. Real speech lies between the two, but the pattern explains why function words are reduced.<span class=\"src\">Photo credits: (a) Vincent Quach, CC BY-SA 3.0.</span></figcaption></figure></p>\n<div class=\"hk\"><h2 id=\"s3\">Formula and fact sheet</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Fact</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RP phonemes</td>\n<td>20 vowels (12 + 8) + 24 consonants = 44</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>schwa</td>\n<td>/ə/, unstressed only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-tion words</td>\n<td>stress on the syllable before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>noun / verb pair</td>\n<td><em>PREsent</em> / <em>preSENT</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sentence stress</td>\n<td>content words, not function words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/θ/ vs /ð/</td>\n<td>thin vs this</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"chsum\"><h2 id=\"s4\">Chapter summary</h2>\n<ul class=\"cols2\">\n<li>English has 44 phonemes in RP; a silent letter adds no sound.</li>\n<li>Words ending in <em>-tion, -ic, -ity</em> stress the preceding syllable.</li>\n<li>Two-syllable nouns take first-syllable stress, verbs second.</li>\n<li>Weak function words reduce to schwa; content words carry sentence stress.</li>\n</ul>\n</section><h2 id=\"s5\" class=\"qs\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Set A — Recall (12 questions)</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"mcq\">\n<li data-key=\"c\">The number of vowel phonemes (including diphthongs) in Received Pronunciation is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">5</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">12</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">20</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">24</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">The number of consonant phonemes in English is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">21</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">24</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">26</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">20</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">The number of syllables in \"beautiful\" is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">3</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">4</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">2</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">5</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">The word \"procrastination\" has its primary stress on the ___ syllable.<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">2nd</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">3rd</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">5th</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">4th</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">Which pair has the same initial vowel sound?<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">up, on</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">ago, alone</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">inn, eel</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">owl, all</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">Which word contains the voiceless /θ/ sound?<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">thin</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">this</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">then</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">bathe</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">Which word contains the /ʃ/ sound?<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">chair</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">measure</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">judge</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">ship</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"c\">In which word is the letter \"b\" silent?<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">bubble</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">rubber</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">climb</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">bring</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">Which word contains a diphthong?<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">bit</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">bed</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">cut</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">boy</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">The noun \"present\" is stressed on the<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">second syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">first syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">either syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">neither syllable</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">In a sentence, stress usually falls on<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">content words</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">articles</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">prepositions</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">auxiliaries</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"c\">Which word has the long vowel /uː/?<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">put</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">foot</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">food</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">good</span></span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"ans box note\"><summary>Answers and explanations — Set A</summary><div class=\"ans-body\">\n<p class=\"ansline\"><strong>1.</strong> (c) — 12 monophthongs + 8 diphthongs.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>2.</strong> (b) — 24 consonant phonemes.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>3.</strong> (a) — beau-ti-ful.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>4.</strong> (d) — pro-cras-ti-<strong>NA</strong>-tion; stress falls before <em>-tion</em>.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>5.</strong> (b) — both begin with the schwa /ə/.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>6.</strong> (a) — <em>thin</em> has /θ/; <em>this, then, bathe</em> have /ð/.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>7.</strong> (d) — <em>ship</em> /ʃ/; <em>measure</em> has /ʒ/; <em>chair</em> /tʃ/; <em>judge</em> /dʒ/.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>8.</strong> (c) — <em>climb</em> /klaɪm/.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>9.</strong> (d) — <em>boy</em> /ɔɪ/.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>10.</strong> (b) — noun: PREsent; verb: preSENT.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>11.</strong> (a) — content words carry the stress.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>12.</strong> (c) — <em>food</em> /fuːd/; <em>put, foot, good</em> have /ʊ/.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Set B — Understanding (6 questions)</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"mcq\">\n<li data-key=\"b\">In \"photographer\" the primary stress is on the<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">1st syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">2nd syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">3rd syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">4th syllable</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"c\">The number of syllables in \"communication\" is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">4</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">6</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">5</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">3</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"a\">Which word contains the /ʒ/ sound?<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">vision</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">vicious</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">visit</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">vivid</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"b\">The words \"ship\" and \"sheep\" differ in<span class=\"opts c2\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">a consonant</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">a vowel</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">stress</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">number of syllables</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"d\">In \"psychology\" the silent letter is<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">y</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">s</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">l</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">p</span></span></span></li>\n<li data-key=\"c\">The word \"economy\" has primary stress on the<span class=\"opts c4\"><span class=\"opt\"><i>a</i><span class=\"ob\">1st syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>b</i><span class=\"ob\">3rd syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>c</i><span class=\"ob\">2nd syllable</span></span><span class=\"opt\"><i>d</i><span class=\"ob\">4th syllable</span></span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"ans box note\"><summary>Answers and explanations — Set B</summary><div class=\"ans-body\">\n<p class=\"ansline\"><strong>1.</strong> (b) — pho-<strong>TOG</strong>-ra-pher: the stress is on the second syllable, as in pho-<strong>TOG</strong>-ra-phy.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>2.</strong> (c) — com-mu-ni-ca-tion.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>3.</strong> (a) — <em>vision</em> /ˈvɪʒn/; <em>vicious</em> has /ʃ/.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>4.</strong> (b) — /ɪ/ versus /iː/: a minimal pair.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>5.</strong> (d) — the initial <em>p</em> is not pronounced: /saɪˈkɒlədʒi/.</p><p class=\"ansline\"><strong>6.</strong> (c) — e-<strong>CON</strong>-o-my.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Group D — Model answers for long questions (10 marks each, for deeper revision)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe the English phonemes (vowels and consonants), with the classification of consonants and phonetic transcription of words. <span class=\"marks\">[10]</span></li>\n<li>Explain syllables, word stress and sentence stress, with rules and examples. <span class=\"marks\">[10]</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><figure class=\"nobreak photo grid\"><table class=\"pgrid\"><tbody><tr><td class=\"gc\"><img class=\"gp\" src=\"/static/web/study/4d9daa3401ef44466b8c.webp\" alt=\"photograph\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(a)</b> Spectrogram</div></td><td class=\"gc\"><img class=\"gp\" src=\"/static/web/study/c88fa727994faea1e11e.webp\" alt=\"photograph\"><div class=\"gcap\"><b>(b)</b> Metronome</div></td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption><b>Plate 3.H.</b> Sound made visible and audible. (a) A spectrogram of the vowel in see, where each dark band is a formant, a frequency the vocal tract strengthens. (b) A metronome ticks at an even rate, as the stressed syllables of English approximately do.<span class=\"src\">Photo credits: (a) Lee Ball, CC0; (b) Vincent Quach, CC BY-SA 3.0.</span></figcaption></figure></p>\n<h3>Model answer D1: The English phonemes (10 marks)</h3>\n<p><strong>Introduction.</strong> English spelling is not phonetic, so we count <strong>sounds</strong> and not letters. A <strong>phoneme</strong> is the smallest unit of sound that can change meaning: <em>ship</em> /ʃɪp/ and <em>sheep</em> /ʃiːp/ differ only in one phoneme.</p><p class=\"ck\">Received Pronunciation (RP) has <strong>44 phonemes</strong>: <strong>20 vowels</strong> and <strong>24 consonants</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Vowels (20).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>12 monophthongs (pure vowels):</strong> short /ɪ e æ ʌ ɒ ʊ ə/ and long /iː ɑː ɔː uː ɜː/. Examples: <em>sit</em> /sɪt/, <em>bed</em> /bed/, <em>cat</em> /kæt/, <em>cup</em> /kʌp/, <em>hot</em> /hɒt/, <em>put</em> /pʊt/, <em>about</em> /əˈbaʊt/, <em>see</em> /siː/, <em>car</em> /kɑː/, <em>saw</em> /sɔː/, <em>blue</em> /bluː/, <em>bird</em> /bɜːd/.</li>\n<li><strong>8 diphthongs (glides):</strong> /eɪ aɪ ɔɪ əʊ aʊ ɪə eə ʊə/. Examples: <em>day</em> /deɪ/, <em>my</em> /maɪ/, <em>boy</em> /bɔɪ/, <em>go</em> /ɡəʊ/, <em>now</em> /naʊ/, <em>here</em> /hɪə/, <em>air</em> /eə/, <em>tour</em> /tʊə/.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Consonants (24)</strong> are described by <strong>manner</strong> and <strong>voicing</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Manner</th>\n<th>Voiceless</th>\n<th>Voiced</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plosives</td>\n<td>/p, t, k/</td>\n<td>/b, d, ɡ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Affricates</td>\n<td>/tʃ/ (church)</td>\n<td>/dʒ/ (judge)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fricatives</td>\n<td>/f, θ, s, ʃ, h/</td>\n<td>/v, ð, z, ʒ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasals</td>\n<td>–</td>\n<td>/m, n, ŋ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lateral, approximants</td>\n<td>–</td>\n<td>/l, r, w, j/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Notes.</strong> A sound is <strong>voiced</strong> when the vocal cords vibrate (put a finger on the throat while saying /z/ and /s/). The fricatives /θ/ (<em>thin</em>) and /ð/ (<em>this</em>) are formed with the tongue tip between the teeth, and Nepali speakers often replace them with /t̪/ and /d̪/.</p>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Transcription practice.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Transcription</th>\n<th>Phonemes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>thought</td>\n<td>/θɔːt/</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>measure</td>\n<td>/ˈmeʒə/</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>church</td>\n<td>/tʃɜːtʃ/</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sing</td>\n<td>/sɪŋ/</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>judge</td>\n<td>/dʒʌdʒ/</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>box</td>\n<td>/bɒks/</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The word <em>box</em> has three letters but four sounds, while <em>sing</em> has four letters but three sounds, which shows that letters and phonemes are different.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The phoneme chart is the key to correct pronunciation and to dictionary symbols, and minimal pairs prove that each phoneme is distinct.</p>\n<h3>Model answer D2: Syllables and stress (10 marks)</h3>\n<p><strong>Introduction.</strong> A <strong>syllable</strong> is a unit of speech with one vowel sound, with or without consonants round it. <em>Book</em> has one syllable, <em>teacher</em> two (/ˈtiː.tʃə/), <em>information</em> four (/ˌɪn.fəˈmeɪ.ʃən/). <strong>Stress</strong> is the extra force given to a syllable.</p>\n<p class=\"kn\"><strong>Word stress rules.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Two-syllable nouns and adjectives</strong> are usually stressed on the first syllable: <em>TAble, PRESent</em> (noun), <em>HAPpy</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-syllable verbs</strong> are usually stressed on the second: <em>preSENT</em> (verb), <em>deCIDE</em>, <em>beGIN</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Noun–verb pairs</strong> change stress: <em>REcord</em> (noun) / <em>reCORD</em> (verb), <em>IMport</em> / <em>imPORT</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compound nouns</strong> take stress on the first part: <em>BLACKboard, GREENhouse</em>; <strong>compound verbs</strong> on the second: <em>underSTAND</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Words ending in -tion, -sion, -ic, -ical, -ial</strong> are stressed on the syllable before the ending: <em>eduCAtion, ecoNOmic, poLItical, ofFIcial</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Words ending in -ity, -graphy, -logy</strong> are stressed on the third syllable from the end: <em>aBILity, phoTOgraphy, bioLOgy</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prefixes and suffixes</strong> such as <em>un-, -ness, -ing</em> usually do not move the stress: <em>HAPpy → unHAPpy</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Sentence stress and rhythm.</strong> In connected speech the <strong>content words</strong> (nouns, main verbs, adjectives, adverbs) are stressed and the <strong>function words</strong> (articles, prepositions, auxiliaries) are weak and often contain /ə/.</p><p class=\"ck\">English is <strong>stress-timed</strong>, so the two sentences below, which have different numbers of syllables, take nearly equal time between the stressed words:</p><p class=\"sub\"><span class=\"sl\">DOGS chase CATS</span> <em>The DOGS are CHASing the CATS.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Intonation.</strong> A <strong>falling tone</strong> is used for statements, commands and wh-questions (<em>Where do you live?</em>). A <strong>rising tone</strong> is used for yes/no questions (<em>Are you coming?</em>) and for lists that are not finished.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Noun / verb</td>\n<td>First / second syllable</td>\n<td><em>PREsent / preSENT</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ic, -tion</td>\n<td>Syllable before the ending</td>\n<td><em>ecoNOmic</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ity</td>\n<td>Third from the end</td>\n<td><em>aBILity</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wh-question</td>\n<td>Falling tone</td>\n<td><em>Where is it?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yes/no question</td>\n<td>Rising tone</td>\n<td><em>Is it here?</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Correct stress and rhythm help a listener understand speech and decide the meaning and the part of speech of a word.</p>"}